main.s01Interactive:
"interactive" — a drawing to move: "drawing" (the same JSON as the page module “Animation”: vars, let, sim, controls, rules with "goal": true, world, elements — as JSON text),
"goal" (the goal expression, e.g. "abs(x - cel) < 0.5 && y <= 0"; empty — the drawing’s rules with "goal"), "solution" ("kat = 45; v0 = sqrt(cel*g)" — a calculation with the drawing’s values; in a world also "w2.pos = 5"; empty — the drawing’s "task.solve", and its "task" draws an equally hard task on every attempt);
checked by simulating the set values to the end (a world — rebuilt by its rules: what is locked stays); values with "random" are drawn on every attempt;
"goals" (instead of "goal"): several goals IN ORDER, e.g. ["x > 3", "y < 1"] — correct only when the first is met first and then the second; "check": "live" (default — the drawing shows a hit already while trying, judged after “Check”) or "submit" (nothing gives the goal away while trying, only “Check” judges);
the import preview computes "solution" to the end and warns when the solution does NOT reach the goal, and lists unknown names in "let", "goal", "goals", "rules" and element expressions; in the drawing’s texts fmt(x, digits) and fmtpi(x) (multiple of π: fmtpi(2.356) → “0.75π”); names of sliders and readouts may contain formulas ($…$), labels on the drawing itself ("text" elements) are plain text (π, ², indices via sub()/sup())

main.s04Tests:
"tests" — what the template’s cards check and how strongly: [{"of": "this" (this entry) | "key of a reference field" (the entries it points to), "weight": 0–10}]; without "tests": this entry with weight 1.
  Knowness belongs to the entry, and a card is evidence of it: a precise question weighs more than a general one, one card can count for several entries;
  e.g. a fact’s card “What happened then?”: "tests": [{"of": "this", "weight": 1}, {"of": "okres", "weight": 0.3}, {"of": "gdzie", "weight": 0.3}]

main.s05Asks:
"asks" — which FACT the template’s cards ask about: [{"of": "this" | "key of a reference field", "facet": "fact name"}] or briefly ["data"]; cards that ask about the same fact of the same entry share every answer
  — e.g. “When was X?” and “What happened in November 1829?” with "asks": ["data"]: a correct answer to one counts for both; in a loop “For each value” the fact is separate for each value; without "asks": the card counts only for itself;
  the pairs of a “Match the pairs” question are facts by themselves: each pair shown counts for its pair’s card

main.s07Generate:
node "generate" (“Generator”) — THE SAME generator as in questions (GENERATOR above): "config": {"params": […], "let": […], "require": […], "texts": [{"name": "pytanie", "value": "What is the formula of {kation.label} with {anion.label}?"}]} — "this" in expressions is this card’s entry (this.symbol); "only": "this.ladunek > 0" — only such entries of the kind get a card (the others without a warning);
  every name (parameter, computed value, text) has its own output with that name: numbers and texts as values, entries as entries, a list as several values — to "prompt" (via "pattern" or directly a text from "texts"), "answer.*" ("expected", "distractors", "items", "left", "right"), "card"
  when the task cannot be drawn (60 attempts), no card is created for this entry — with the reason in the template preview; the card stores the drawn values and the draw number; the card’s history starts again only after the template’s graph changes

main.s20Type:
“Knowness filter”: {"type": "filter.when", "config": {"when": condition}} — cards for which the condition holds stay, checked DURING the revision at every card: what waited comes in when the condition starts to hold; a card already in the stack stays
  the condition is the same logic as in flows: {"type": "knowness", "rel": "before", "band": "learned"} — what must be known earlier has been learned;
  "band" — a band instead of a number: "learned" (learned: due or known — a due date does not close what comes next) | "known" (known, from 100 %) | "due" (due for revision) | "unknown" (unknown); or a number: "op": "ge", "than": 0.5 (the old "rel": "before" / "up" with "than": 1 is read as "learned");
  "rel": "self" (this entry) | "cards" (its cards; in a revision: this card) | "before" (what must be known earlier — the nearest entries with cards in learning order) | "up" (what it lies under in the structure) | "chain" (already asked cards of the WHOLE way before it — through all steps, also through entries without questions and circles; never-asked ones never count) | "core" (the topic’s key questions — questions with "core": true that list it in "about"; in a revision: of this card’s topics) | key of a reference field (what it points to, e.g. "okres");
  gaps of a topic (a key question already asked and now unknown): {"type": "knowness", "rel": "core", "each": "any", "band": "unknown"} — e.g. “Fill the gaps” takes cards of topics whose key question is unknown;
  the landscape lock as a condition for any view and revision: {"type": "knowness", "rel": "chain", "band": "known"} — every already asked card on the whole way before the entry is known (nothing in it fades, nothing was recently wrong);
  "each": "all" (default — every one) | "any" (at least one); when there is nothing of the kind, "all" holds; "of": "@wpis" instead of "rel" — the knowness of one entry; conditions are combined with "and" / "or" / "not": {"type": "and", "items": [ … ]}

main.s30Type:
steps ("type") and their outputs:
  "show" {"of": "@wpis", "title", "media", "drawing"} → "next" — shows an entry (a statement with “Content in blocks” in full, other entries with their fields); the step’s title: the step’s "title", without it — the name of the slide shown (its label, e.g. the field “tytul”); in a block paragraph an empty line starts a new paragraph, a single line break breaks the line; a quote is a paragraph with lines “> …” and a last “> — Author”, footnotes [^1] in the text and “[^1]: …” at the end (the “Footnotes” list is created by itself — the import warns about “Quote (…):”, a hand-written “Footnotes” list, “click next” and a quote without “> —”); the learner’s place in the story lies in their progress (flow position: step, visited, completed, goals — also on other devices); after an import that changes step identifiers, only nonexistent visited steps disappear, the player returns to the first unvisited step from the beginning, “completed”, goals and walked paths stay (stable step "id"s — e.g. "s<klucz>" — keep the place); "drawing" — A DRAWING NEXT TO THE SLIDE TEXT (wide: text on the left, drawing on the right; on a phone below the text): the same JSON as the page module “Animation” and the question "interactive" (vars, let, controls — sliders, elements …), static or with sliders, without goal and without grading; as an object, JSON text or the key of a field of the shown entry that holds the drawing (e.g. "drawing": "rysunek" — a field of type "json"/"text"), e.g. {"id": "s7", "type": "show", "config": {"of": "@laag/folie/zwei-geraden", "drawing": {"vars": [{"name": "m", "value": 1, "min": -3, "max": 3, "step": 0.5}], "controls": [{"type": "slider", "var": "m", "label": "Steigung $m$"}], "elements": [{"type": "plot", "fn": "m*x + 1", "from": -5, "to": 5}, {"type": "plot", "fn": "-x + 2", "from": -5, "to": 5}]}}};
  "question" {"of": "@pytanie"} → "right" | "wrong" — a question from the base; the answer lies on ITS card: it counts for what it is about ("about"), and together with revisions; THE PLAYER ASKS ONLY WHAT THE LEARNER DOES NOT KNOW: a question whose card is already known (from 100 %, from the whole history — stories, revisions, practice) is skipped silently via the output "right" — without an answer and without changing the card’s plan; slides are always shown; do not add conditions for this — it applies to every base and every flow (a review with only known questions ends at once, the story can then be read); after a wrong answer the same question does not come back right away — first a known one (a “spacer”, it does not count and changes nothing, unless the answer is wrong);
  "choice" {"title", "of", "options": [{"label", "hint", "condition", "actions"}], "hold"} → "o0", "o1", … — a choice (“Which way next?”): the story stops and shows ALL options by name and with what waits behind each (“3 slides to read · 5 questions to answer”); an option whose "condition" is not met stands closed and says what will open it (with a "not" condition — why it is closed); the app never chooses by itself; paths may return to the choice (then they stay open for later); (2026-11-11) "of" — a slide entry as in a "show" step: its title and text (paragraphs, quotes, footnotes, links, formulas, "drawing") stand ABOVE the options on ONE screen, and the choice's "title" then heads the options; a choice with "of" never chooses by itself (as with "hold": true); "hint" — one short line under the option's name (plain text with inline markup; over 120 characters: a warning); "label" stays the short name of the branch (the line name on the world map); do not put a separate slide before a choice only to describe the options;
  "explore" {"scope": ["@area|@tower|@unit", …], "downTo", "modes": ["adaptive", "target"], "default", "targetLevels", "streak", "errors", "recheck", "once", "push", "title", "of"} → "done" — (2026-11-12) PLACEMENT („Check how much you already know”): checks the not yet conquered towers of the scope with real questions of the base and moves on to the towers that build on them; "adaptive": "streak" right in a row (1–10, default 3) credits a tower (credit = level "openAt", at least 1; without levels: conquered), "errors" wrong (default 2) in one tower end it for this run and recheck its prerequisites credited in this run ("recheck" right in a row, default 2; a wrong answer withdraws the credit and goes further down); "target": towers asked like a real conquest of the chosen level from "targetLevels" (only with levels and when the service stores runs); "downTo" — the whole base of a tower with it; "once" (default true) — done after its end, later „Check again”; "push" (default true) — the choice option whose path leads to this step stands first as “Recommended”;
  "chapters" {"title", "chapters": [{"title"}]} → "c0", "c1", … and "done" — A FORK THAT JOINS AGAIN: shows all paths with what waits behind each; done ones (in this story, or ones whose every step the learner already knows) stand as “completed”, “Completed 2 of 3 paths”, and when all are behind — it goes via the output "done"; "chapterEnd" — the end of a path (returns to the choice); "restart" — this path again;
  "condition" {"condition": condition} → "yes" | "no" — without a screen, e.g. “skip if known”: {"type": "knowness", "of": "@temat", "band": "known"}; on the path it is NEVER the learner’s choice: only the branch that applies now is drawn, and when that is not known yet — both together as one part with the mark “automatic”; a path that returns to the choice has a short arrow back on the path; the path has one “End” at the top, and above it what comes after this story in the learning order (no hand-made links); "set" {"actions": [{"type": "set"|"add", "value", "to"|"by"}]} → "next";
  "expand" {"of", "threshold"} → "next" (explains while not yet known); "opening" {"mode": "discovery"|"recall"}; "line" {"character", "of", "mood"}; "subflow" {"flow"}; "each" {"collection"|"kind", "flow"}; "handoff" {"flow"}; "detour" {"flow": "@inna-opowiesc", "label": "Want to know more: …", "optional": true} → "done" | "skip"; "review" {"of": "story", "threshold": 0.9} → "passed" | "notyet" (REVIEW IN ONE STEP — see below); "end"; "return"
  DETOUR ("detour") — arrange stories like a ladder: one general one, under it short reviews of the stages, under them deeper stories; KEEP THE REVIEW SHORT, put deeper content into its OWN story (its own flow, usually with its own "unit") and connect it with a "detour" step with "optional": true where it fits; the player shows a card: the name of that story, what waits in it (“7 slides · 16 questions”), “Enter” and “Continue without it” (when already completed: “you know this”, “Refresh” and “Continue”); “Enter” plays it inside (the title, path and counters are then its own, above it a bar “Stage 1 › The house in Becchi” with “Back to: Stage 1” — return to the detour step without completing), its end returns via the output "done", “Continue without it” goes via the output "skip" (usually both to the same next step); progress belongs to the story: that one is completed once, wherever it was finished, and its questions count as always; a skipped detour blocks nothing, on the path it counts as done (thin outline), and in “What next” after the end of the story it stands first; a story’s review covers only its own questions; "optional": false (rare) — only “Enter”, and the end of the story requires it (then without the output "skip"); at most 3 detours nested; a CIRCLE (A → B → A) is rejected by the import with both names; a deeper story whose "unit" requires (a field with "prerequisite": "up") the unit of the review, opened directly before the review is completed, says “First: Stage 1” — from the review one can always enter; example: {"id": "z1", "type": "detour", "config": {"flow": "@bosko/becchi", "label": "Want to know more: The house in Becchi without a father", "optional": true}}, edges {"from": "z1", "out": "done", "to": "s5"} and {"from": "z1", "out": "skip", "to": "s5"}
  step "scene" {"page": "page/address", "scene": "scene key", "title"} — a presentation scene; outputs "next", with a question on the scene "right" / "wrong", buttons "b0", "b1", …

main.s51Mode:
"mode": "landscape" (Landscape) — the personal knowledge landscape of whoever is looking: ONE MAP of all topics — each topic is one hexagonal column at a fixed place (always the same place; nothing moves on zooming or clicking); REGIONS form by themselves from the links “Must know first” — groups of about 5–12 topics that depend a lot on each other and little on the rest, level by level, until there are about 7–20 regions at the top (no loops; NOT from chapters — the view’s "group" is only a colour without regions); regions only ARRANGE the map: a region’s topics lie together as one patch, subregions as patches within it, regions do not show dependencies (no roads or lines between regions); the same for every learner, recomputed only after the base changes; region name: from the base when it fits (chapter, section “§4 …”), otherwise “Around: <the topic most others in it depend on>”; the owner renames it and moves a topic to another region (menu “⋯” in the topic popup) — this stays after recomputing; places come only from the structure (never from progress), recomputed on every page open (nothing is recomputed while in use), the same for every learner: the HOME on its own field in the very centre (a simple flat little house lying on the field; touching it chooses the starting topic, “Reset view” returns home); starting topic = the most general topic without requirements (the one most builds on), next to home; FOUNDATION = the start, topics that need only it, and topics at least half of the base builds on (in a deep base often a whole region, in a flat one only the start and its direct successors), as a compact round patch around home, divided into region sectors; every other top region starts at the edge of the foundation on the side where its requirements lie and grows outward as one wedge (wider rather than longer), subregions within it — every region is one connected patch; the whole map is one compact, nearly round shape without holes, islands or lone columns; what you know grows around home;
  HEIGHT = STEPS: the longest chain of topics still missing, this topic included — valley 0 (known, also what is due), one step — can be started now, each further step higher; ONE step height for all fields of the base, fitted to the base: the fewer steps the base has (longest chain), the higher each step (a flat base shows clear differences, a deep one looks as before); the CAMERA belongs to the learner and is free: Ctrl + drag (or the right button) rotates smoothly around the centre of the view and tilts: up — more from the side (towards the horizon), down — more from above (a slider with icons “from the side” and “from above”), two fingers rotate, tilt and zoom, dragging pans, a button rotates by 45°, “Reset view”; the view (zoom, place, tilt, rotation) stays until next time; the app never changes the angle by itself; from above everything is visible; the camera stays (this browser, this learner); TOPICS: how many topics still have to be learned, this one included — that many steps has the “step by step” list;
  "landscape": {"nest": "<reference field>", "nestSort": "nr", "regions": {"names": {"<region key>": {"name": "Restklassen", "topics": ["@temat", …]}}, "moves": {"@temat": "<region key>"}}} — "regions" is written by the app: the owner’s names together with the region’s topics (after recomputing a name finds its region by shared topics) and topics moved to another region; "budget" and "bands" from older bases are read and have no effect;
  "nest": NESTED LANDSCAPE — the groups come from the data, not from computation: a map node is an ordinary entry of its own kind (e.g. “Map field”: "nazwa", "nr" — order, a reference field to the node kind with "structure": "up" — the parent node; empty: a top node; branches may have different depths), and a content entry points to its node with a reference field whose key is given in "nest" (without a node it stands at the top level); "nestSort" — a numeric field ordering the nodes (default "nr"); nodes are regions of this one map (patches within patches) — from afar the names of the top nodes, zooming in shows the names of lower ones (lower nodes OR the node’s entries — the entries of a node that also has lower nodes stand in their own region “Others in: …”, so that no map mixes regions with topics), breadcrumbs lead back; a node is a real field: ring = known / all cards of the whole subtree, colour from this knowness (the more is known, the deeper), height = distance to the nearest entry inside that can be learned (0 when something inside is ready), it sinks into the valley when everything inside is mastered (with a revision mark when something is due), a lock when everything inside waiting to be learned needs revision first; node popup: name, ring, state in words, ONE main button (“Enter”, “Revise” — cards due in the subtree, “Set as goal” — the nearest entry inside; at a node that is a story unit — the story button) and a list of what is inside; places are fixed: the fields of a level packed in rings around the learner, closer what must be learned earlier (the earliest entry inside), then nodes before entries and the author’s order — never by progress; a story unit may be a node or a content entry;
  the language of the map: hue = region (each top region has its own hue, safe for colour-blind people, neighbours never similar; zoomed into a region its subregions have neighbouring hues, neighbouring subregions different ones), brightness and saturation belong to knowledge (known — full colour, distant mountains pale), height = steps; NO marks on the fields; above the map a view switch (remembered with the view): “Overview”, “What I know” (only what you know in colour, in an orange-red outline), “To discover” (only what can be learned now in colour, in a light-yellow outline, what is known pale around it), “To revise” (only topics with revision in colour; when nothing — “Nothing to revise”); next to the tilt three column heights: low, medium, high (×0.5 / ×1 / ×2 of the base step, remembered with the view); RING at the edge of a field (from medium zoom, without text): the average knowness of the topic’s cards (90, 90, 90, 100, 100, 100 % → 95 %), from the top clockwise, a dot — something to revise; WHAT YOU KNOW IS YOUR TERRITORY — one orange-red outline around everything you know, and a light-yellow outline around topics you can learn now; region colours stay fully visible inside; a region where you know everything — conquered (a small flag by its name, “Region conquered: …”); “you are here” is a small quiet dot; PAWNS stand only through your action: opening a topic for learning (double-click on the field, “Start”, “Learn now”) puts a pawn on it that stays until you know the topic; setting a goal puts a flag on the goal and one pawn in the goal’s colour on the way — on the next topic to learn, it moves on when the next topic is known; touching a pawn opens the topic (the story where it was left), a flag — the goal with its way; from afar the pawns of one region as one with a number (touching zooms in); pawns are saved with progress (another device sees the same); above the map one sentence starting with your territory: “Your territory: 4 topics · 1 of 12 regions conquered · 8 ready · 10 to revise” with two buttons; ZOOMING smooth (wheel, two fingers, “+”/“−”, double-click on a region — smoothly to the whole region) and semantic: from afar (and the whole map in view) relief, colours and the name of EVERY top region in its colour, on its own surface (a short line only for a region too small for its name), never disappearing; each level of subregions has its own zoom step: closer the names of subregions, closer still their subregions etc. ( hovering or touching highlights the whole region, others fade; clicking zooms in), without topic names; medium — subregion names in their hue and rings on the fields, without topic names; close — on each field its name (1–2 lines, normal type, no larger than page text) and ring; deepest (2–5 fields in view) — in addition at most one word of state (“known”, “3 to revise”); text lies on the top face (tilts and rotates with it, too steep — disappears) and is drawn together with its column: a column in front hides it, a name on an almost hidden face is not shown; outlines thick where visible, thin and lighter where a column in front covers them; pawns and flags hidden by a column in front leave a small dot at its top edge; breadcrumbs follow the zoom (“Whole map › Chapter I › Around: Relations”), clicking a breadcrumb zooms out to it; the legend is collapsed in “Legend”;
  click: a region (from afar, or its name) — zooms smoothly to it on the same map, double-click on a topic (close) — opens it for learning, at the side its name, numbers and “Continue here” (EVERY topic that can be learned now); a topic (close up) — selects it: on the ground short soft roads to what it requires (darker) and what it opens (lighter), nothing more; a second click starts (when possible); the topic popup is short: name and state in one sentence (“Ready — you can start”, “2 topics still missing”, “To revise: 5 cards”), when something is missing — “First you need:” (at most 5, ready ones first, each with “Learn now”; the name zooms the map to the topic), main button “Lead me here” (sets the goal and starts the first missing step right away) or “Start”, collapsed “Opens next: 5 topics”, for a story one line “Story: 16 parts · 0 completed” with “Show path” (the path in a separate large view, never in the popup), at the bottom one line about the region (“House in a storm: 5 topics · you know 0 of 5”); author tools only in “⋯” and only for those who write in the base; the goal is saved privately like progress;
  e.g. {"ref": "@swiat/w-krajobraz", "name": "Knowledge landscape", "mode": "landscape", "steps": [{"type": "source.kind", "config": {"kind": "@swiat/temat"}}], "group": {"by": "axis", "kind": "@swiat/tag", "via": ["tagi"]}}; nested: node kind {"ref": "@swiat/pole", "name": "Map field", "label": "{nazwa}", "fields": [{"key": "nazwa", "type": "line"}, {"key": "nr", "type": "number"}, {"key": "nad", "type": "ref", "to": ["@swiat/pole"], "structure": "up"}]}, nodes {"ref": "@swiat/m-ruch", "kinds": ["@swiat/pole"], "data": {"nazwa": "Motion", "nr": 1, "nad": "@swiat/m-fizyka"}}, a topic with the field {"key": "pole", "type": "ref", "to": ["@swiat/pole"]} → "data": {"pole": "@swiat/m-ruch"}, view {"ref": "@swiat/w-pola", "name": "Field landscape", "mode": "landscape", "steps": [{"type": "source.kind", "config": {"kind": "@swiat/temat"}}], "landscape": {"nest": "pole"}}

head.s04Anchors:
"{schemaFormat}" — kinds with their card templates and add forms (an independent copy for another knowledge base); the schema export contains no views, but the import accepts them in every format, as it does "anchors"
  a schema may carry READY-MADE views and revisions ("views", "revisions") — the import creates them like any others, the user copies and changes them; with a lasting name ("@fakty/…") the next schema import updates them;
  e.g. templates/cogita/fakty.json carries the view and revision “From general to specific”: {"views": [{"ref": "@fakty/w-ogolne", "name": "From general to specific", "steps": [{"type": "source.kind", "config": {"kind": "@czas/okres"}}, {"type": "source.kind", "config": {"kind": "@fakty/fakt"}}, {"type": "filter.when", "config": {"when": {"type": "knowness", "rel": "before", "op": "ge", "than": 1}}}]}], "revisions": [{"ref": "@fakty/p-ogolne", "name": "From general to specific", "graph": …}]}

head.s19:
OTHER WORLDS (references to another knowledge base — an offer, never a requirement): "@@<base name>:<@lasting/name or identifier>", e.g. "@@historia:@historia/rewolucja-1688" — an entry, story (flow) or view of that base;
  the base name is its lasting name from its Settings: {"settings": {"handle": "historia"}} (lowercase letters, digits, “-”, “_”; it stays after the base is renamed);
  a base that refers to others knows them in its Settings: {"settings": {"handle": "fizyka", "worlds": [{"id": "<identifier of that base>", "handle": "historia", "name": "History"}]}} — the import adds the world by itself when it finds a base with this name among the bases you read;
  where: in a reference field with "external": true (kind: {"key": "swiaty", "label": "In other worlds", "type": "ref", "multi": true, "external": true}, entry: "swiaty": ["@@historia:@historia/rewolucja-1688", "@@historia:@historia/f/rewolucja"]),
  in text markup: [[revolution of 1688|@@historia:@historia/rewolucja-1688]], as a detour: {"id": "z2", "type": "detour", "config": {"flow": "@@historia:@historia/f/rewolucja", "label": "Detour into another world: History"}} (always optional; outputs "done" and "skip");
  the import accepts them everywhere and nothing stops it because of them — it checks them in that base and warns (unknown world, a world you do not read, no such entry, a detour to something that is not a story, "@@…" in a field without "external": true); the export writes them in the same form;
  they are never a requirement: a field with "external": true cannot have "prerequisite" or "structure", and "@@…" in a "prerequisite" field is ignored — they do not count towards knowness, landscape, goals or revisions of this base; that base decides by itself what comes earlier in it.

kinds.s03Reading:
  "reading" — reading text: how the learner sees an entry of this kind (“New topic” before the first question about this entry, the explanation after answering) — markup with fields in braces, like "label",
    e.g. "{streszczenie}

{opis}

**Events**
{wydarzenia}

*{czas}*"; the first paragraph is the lead (short; the rest after “Read more”);
    a "markup" field stays markup (bold, footnotes [@dzielo, p. 12]), "text" — text, a "list" alone on a line — bullet points (under a bold subheading it is collapsed until the learner opens it),
    "ref" — a link with the entry’s name, "ref" with "locator" — a footnote to the source with the place (with “show in text”), date, number, choice — as text; files and places on the map are not shown;
    a part of the pattern up to an empty line in which all fields are empty disappears together with its subheading;
    without "reading": the summary (field "streszczenie" or "summary") or the first paragraph of text, then the text fields, dates and places; "reading": "" removes the pattern

kinds.s07Prerequisite:
"prerequisite" (reference) — LEARNING ORDER: "up" — what it points to must be known EARLIER (concept → “Requires”: an orbital requires the electron and the atom); "down" — what it points to builds on this entry (“Leads to”);
  from such fields (and from the "needs" field of questions) the app computes levels from the basics: detail cards come when the basics are mastered (a view or revision with a “Knowness filter” "rel": "before" — e.g. the ready-made “Basics first”; Learning → Path), with order "general" new cards go from general to specific;
  this is different from "structure" (what lies under what); a circle (A requires B, B requires A) does not work and is listed in “To do”
  e.g. {"key": "wymaga", "label": "Requires", "type": "ref", "to": ["@pojecie"], "multi": true, "prerequisite": "up", "inverse": "Builds on this"}

kinds.s10Quantity:
"quantity" (number) — which quantity (QUDT identifier, e.g. "Torque", "Mass"); then the value is entered in any unit of this quantity and saved as entered, together with SI:
  {"value": 1.5, "unit": "KiloN", "si": 1500} ("unit" — QUDT unit identifier: "N", "KiloN", "N-M", "J", "KiloGM", "DEG_C" …; a number without a unit — in the field’s unit);
  e.g. {"key": "moment", "label": "Torque", "type": "number", "unit": "N·m", "quantity": "Torque"}, and in the entry: "moment": {"value": 12, "unit": "N-M", "si": 12};
  without "quantity" the field takes the quantity from the unit when it is unambiguous ("kg" → Mass; "N·m" — torque or energy — must be chosen)

kinds.s16Blocks:
"blocks" (content in blocks) — a list of blocks: {"type": "paragraph", "text": "markup"} | {"type": "ordered"|"unordered", "items": [cell, …]} | {"type": "table", "columns": ["heading", …], "rows": [[cell, …], …]} | {"type": "details", "summary": "Beweisidee", "text": "markup"} (expandable — collapsed with the heading "summary");
  a cell is a list of pieces: {"text": "…"} or {"ref": "@wpis", "locator": "Łk 1,35"} — a real reference (to an entry of this base, another base or a library source), with or without a place
  every kind with a "blocks" field is a STATEMENT kind (e.g. Comment): thesis = the first sentence of the first paragraph (at most {thesisLimit} characters) — the entry’s name and the first side of the card;
  a thesis ending with “?” makes the entry a question — the answer is a statement that refers to it; “information” (refers only to library sources) or “interpretation” (to another statement or to no source) is computed from the references and not stored

body.s01:
common to all: {shared};
  "about": ["@wpis"] — what the question checks: its result counts towards the knowness of these entries, and the question waits until you master what must be known before them;
  "needs": ["@wpis or @pytanie"] — what must be mastered before this question (a general question before a specific one)
  "core": true — the key question of the topics in "about": “Check what you know” asks it once (a topic without a key question: its first question), after it a “gap” becomes visible (filter “Gaps” on the landscape, condition "rel": "core"), the card carries the mark “key”, and in the topic panel it stands at the top; e.g. {"ref": "@q/becchi-1", "kinds": ["question"], "data": {"form": "one", "prompt": "Where was John Bosco born?", "options": ["Becchi", "Turin", "Chieri"], "correct": "Becchi", "about": ["@t/becchi"], "core": true}}

body.s12Build:
"build" — the tiles of the correct answer in order in "items", extra ones in "extra", other correct orders in "alternatives" (a list of lists), "preview": "math" (the whole as one formula; a $…$ tile goes in without dollars) or "text", "join" — what stands between tiles (default a space);
  e.g. {"form": "build", "prompt": "Bilden Sie die Negation von $\\forall a\\in A: P(a)$.", "items": ["$\\exists a\\in A$", "$:$", "$\\neg P(a)$"], "extra": ["$\\forall a\\in A$", "$P(a)$"], "preview": "math"}
  "order" with "preview" shows the arranged items together above the list (like "build"); "extra" with "order" changes nothing — changing "form" to "build" turns them on

body.s14Generate:
GENERATOR — PARAMETERS (every type; the task is drawn anew each time the card is shown; a template has the same mechanism in the node "generate"):
  "params" — drawn in order: {"name": "a", "random": [20, 99], "step": 1} (a number from the range; whole bounds — step 1) · {"name": "f", "values": ["sin", "cos"]} (one from the list; an item may be a list)
    · {"name": "kation", "entry": {"kind": "@rodzaj", "where": "it.ladunek > 0"}} — one entry from the base · {"name": "zd", "entries": {"kind": "@rodzaj", "count": 4}} — several DIFFERENT ones (without "count": all matching, in their order);
    "where" — an expression with "it" (the candidate), may read earlier parameters (an anion matching the cation); "rule" — a rule as in collections ({"op": "and", "items": [{"field": "data", "cmp": "filled"}]}); an entry already drawn in this task does not come back ("repeat": true — it may);
    a drawn entry is a record: kation.label, kation.id, kation.kind and every field by key — number (with a quantity: field_si in SI, field_unit), text, choice (field_label — the name), yes/no as 1/0, date as a number yyyymmdd for comparisons (field_text — in words), reference as a record one step further (zd[0].okres.label)
  "let": [{"name": "n", "value": "lcm(kation.ladunek, -anion.ladunek)"}] — computed in order (expression language below); "require": ["isint(x)", "a != b"] — draws again (up to 60 times) until all are met; if impossible — the card is NOT created (never broken), and the author sees the reason in the editor, in “To do” and in the import preview
  in texts (prompt, options, answers, items, left, right, back, hints, explanation, feedback, extra, choices) { expression } is a value — numbers with a decimal comma, in a formula {,}, an entry — its name, a list — comma-separated;
    a whole list item as one list: "options": ["{dobra}", "{zle}"] — when {zle} is a list, each of its items is a separate option (likewise items, left, right, answers); "correct": ["{k}"], "value": "{a.data < b.data}" (true/false), "tolerance": "{tol}" — computed too;
    {…} with a name that is not a parameter is plain text (LaTeX: \mathbb{N}, x^{2}); to use a parameter letter as a LaTeX group, write it with spaces: \frac{ a }{2}
  the answer stores the draw number ("seed") and the drawn values (entries as "@id", short lists); the card stays the same (the question key) — its history starts again ONLY when the generator (params, let, require) or the answer pattern (answers, options, correct, items …) changes, never on a draw
  "rule" with form "text": {"spaces": true} — spaces do not count; {"scripts": true} — indices (₂, ²) like ordinary digits: Al2(SO4)3 = Al₂(SO₄)₃
  e.g. {"form": "number", "prompt": "Bestimmen Sie $r$ mit $0\\le r<{n}$ und ${a} = q\\cdot {n} + r$.", "params": [{"name": "a", "random": [20, 99]}, {"name": "n", "random": [3, 9]}], "let": [{"name": "r", "value": "mod(a, n)"}], "answers": ["{r}"]}
  e.g. {"form": "number", "prompt": "A car travelled {s} m in {t} s. What was its average speed?", "params": [{"name": "s", "random": [20, 500], "step": 10}, {"name": "t", "random": [2, 60]}], "let": [{"name": "v", "value": "s / t"}], "answers": ["{v}"], "unit": "m/s", "quantity": "Velocity", "tolerance": 0.01, "relative": true}
  e.g. {"form": "one", "prompt": "What came first?", "params": [{"name": "a", "entry": {"kind": "@hist/zdarzenie"}}, {"name": "b", "entry": {"kind": "@hist/zdarzenie", "where": "it.data != a.data"}}], "let": [{"name": "pierwsze", "value": "minby([a, b], it.data)"}, {"name": "drugie", "value": "maxby([a, b], it.data)"}], "options": ["{pierwsze.label}", "{drugie.label}"], "correct": [0]}

usage.text:
From the answers the app computes how well you know things — in the browser, the same everywhere:
- each card: when last answered, how much time really passed before the answer, the interval until next time (right: that time × 3; partly: a third, at least a day if days have passed; wrong: 5 minutes; right just after a mistake: 0.4 × the best interval before the mistake) and its knowness, which drops over time;
- each entry: the average knowness of its answered cards, weighted by how strongly each checks it (the template in the schema), plus what builds on it;
- from that: which cards a revision gives now and why (the reason is shown at every card), what views and revisions with a “Knowness filter” show (e.g. details of a period only once the period is known), “Path”, “Progress” and the reminder of the next revision — the service only sees when it is and how many cards are waiting;
- all answers are kept: when the way of computing changes, everything can be recomputed; a card whose correct answer changed counts only answers since that change.
The import adds answers only to your progress; the course leader sees them like answers from learning.
